Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 06:57:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] panvk: keep the raw blocks of readable BC lowmem images in a
 spillable store

csf-v11/197 had to keep the raw BC block plane of every lowmem image with
TRANSFER_SRC usage in the image memory, because Vulkan returns those
blocks from image -> buffer copies and BC-source image copies. DXVK sets
TRANSFER_SRC on every texture, so under DXVK the plane cost most of the
lowmem saving (BC1-BC5 images were 2x the BC size, BC6H/BC7 unchanged).

Such images (PANVK_BC_RAW=store, the default, raw blocks of at least
PANVK_BC_RAW_MIN bytes, default 64 KiB) now get no raw plane in the image
memory: vkGetImageMemoryRequirements reports the shadow only, the size
of the RC4-lowmem build (BC1/BC4 = BC size, BC3/BC5 = BC size, BC7 = the
8 bpp AFRC shadow, BC6H = the exact shadow). The raw blocks go to a
per-image panvk_bc_raw:

 - a driver BO (CPU-cached mapping) holds them while command buffers that
   touch raw blocks exist. Every raw access (buffer -> image upload,
   image -> buffer copy, both sides of vkCmdCopyImage) takes a reference
   for the command buffer at record time (panvk_bc_raw_acquire()); the
   reference goes when the command buffer is reset or freed, which it can
   only be once it is no longer pending. planes[0] points at the BO, so
   all the existing raw paths work unchanged and the GPU sees a single
   copy of the blocks whatever the submission order;
 - when no command buffer references the store, a low-priority worker
   thread writes the blocks to an unlinked per-device spill file (O_TMPFILE
   in $PANVK_BC_RAW_DIR, $TMPDIR, the Android app cache directory
   /data/user/<user>/<package>/cache, /data/local/tmp or /tmp; app
   processes can not write /data/local/tmp), drops the page
   cache copy and frees the BO. The copy only commits if no acquire came in
   meanwhile (generation count);
 - the next raw access reads them back at record time into a new BO. An
   image that never got blocks gets a zeroed BO (that is also what a
   ZERO_INITIALIZED image holds; its decode reads a zero row). A store read
   back twice stays resident (no ping-pong for images that are updated or
   copied all the time). Without a spill directory stores stay resident,
   which costs what the in-image plane did.

Uploads transcode or decode from the copy source as before, so the store
is never read on the upload path. An upload or BC -> BC image copy whose
regions cover every block of the image (they can not overlap) gets a new
BO without the read-back or the zero fill and CPU cache flush. Images that are destroyed while command
buffers still hold the store free it with the last reference. Images
without TRANSFER_SRC keep the 197 behaviour (no raw blocks at all), and
PANVK_BC_RAW=plane restores the in-image plane.

Trade-offs: raw blocks are written to flash once per upload (about the
BC size of every texture DXVK uploads, freed with the image by punching
holes in the file), and a later raw read costs a pread at record time.
Both only happen for TRANSFER_SRC images; the GPU-side cost is unchanged.

